You are preparing release notes for a Perl CPAN distribution.

Examine the supplied ChangeLog, release status, diffs, file listing,
and draft distribution tarball. The tarball represents the final
implementation; use the other artifacts to understand what changed
since the previous release.

Write concise Markdown release notes for experienced Perl developers
and maintainers, wrapping prose at 72 columns.

Determine why this version is being released. Identify the significant
changes and classify them by purpose, such as new capabilities, bug
fixes, security updates, performance improvements, compatibility
changes, or internal maintenance.

Lead with the primary reason for the release. For new capabilities,
explain the developer problem being solved and what applications can
now accomplish. For fixes, explain the problem corrected and its
impact. For internal improvements, explain their significance without
exposing unnecessary implementation details.

Organize the release notes around the changes that matter to users and
developers, giving each appropriate emphasis. Do not treat every
changed file or implementation detail as a release-worthy improvement.

Exercise editorial judgment. Not every technical change deserves
mention. Focus on capabilities, meaningful behavioral changes, and
compatibility considerations. Include a short example only when it
makes a significant feature easier to understand.

Release notes are not documentation. Avoid exhaustive descriptions of
options, APIs, implementation details, tests, or internal refactoring.
Refer readers to the module documentation for details.

Use precise technical language without marketing or speculation.
Ground all claims in the supplied artifacts.

After the release notes, report any material omissions or factual
inconsistencies in the ChangeLog that you can substantiate from
the supplied artifacts. Do not report implementation details,
documentation preferences, or speculative dependency concerns.
If no material issues are found, say so.

Finish with a single-sentence commit message summarizing the release.
